#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124217 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/id #uuid "4b1227cb-7cf9-534d-a22f-e7acf7df9dc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4830" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true if and only if $x_1=x_2=0$. So, the set is linearly independent.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry $a_{11} = 0$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124218 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "91989c25-1d0d-5639-b3e1-7f7479322b7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4831" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n2. $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ spans $V$\n3. $\\left\\{-\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V$, and\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n$x_{1}b_{2} + x_{2}(-b_{1}) + x_{3}(b_{3} + b_{1})|= 0$\nis equivalent to\n$x_{1}b_{2} + (x_{3} - x_{2})b_{1} + x_{3}b_{3}|= 0$,\nwhich, since $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n${x_{1} = 0; x_{3} - x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0$\nAlso, $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$:\n$x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+x_3\\mathbf{b}_3 = x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+(x_3-x_1)(-\\mathbf{b}_1)+x_3 (\\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1})$\n- Statement II is false. In order for $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ to span $V$, every element of $\\mathcal{B}$ must be able to be expressed as a linear combination of elements of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$. But the vector $\\mathbf{b}_1 \\in \\mathcal{B}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the elements of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$.\n- Statement III is false. If $\\left\\{-\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ not both zero such that\n$x_1(-\\mathbf{b_2}) + x_2(3\\mathbf{b_1}) = \\mathbf{0}$.\nThen, we would have\n$(3x_2) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_1} + (-x_1) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_2} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_3= \\mathbf{0}$.\nBut this would mean that $\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent, which contradicts the fact that $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis of $V$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124321 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] :question/id #uuid "92adb651-e2db-5296-817d-aea249afe2b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8269" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degrees at most $2$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\big\\{0,t^2 \\big\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$\n2. $-5t^2$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\big\\{0, t^2 \\big\\}$\n3. $\\big\\{0, t^2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n$1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0$,\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$-5t^2 = 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + (-5) \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2}$.\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95b75818-319b-5914-b99c-55668693f607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121655} {:answer/id #uuid "a801a3b4-8fca-5459-950b-caa681c6dd47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121656} {:answer/id #uuid "357b8fa9-f613-5e5b-b8ac-10a32d015795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121657} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0fee84-f4b4-586b-9187-0218625fce1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121658} {:answer/id #uuid "4f209759-5e90-5804-964a-7fac1610bbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95b75818-319b-5914-b99c-55668693f607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121655} :answer-field/id #uuid "f87bfadf-b04d-55f0-86db-1fa04135a1bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121654}] :question/id #uuid "6d5104d0-4e4f-5680-8c4e-7e5a0379f267" :question/math-academy-id "q-34638" :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ and $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}, c_{3}}$ be bases of $R^{2}$ and $R^{3}$, respectively.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. ${b_{1}, b_{2}, x}$ is a basis of $R^{2}$\n2. ${b_{1}, b_{2}}$ is a basis of $R^{3}$\n3. ${c_{3}, c_{2}, c_{1}}$ is a basis of $R^{3}$"}]}
